the government hopes that tough  enforcement of the rules  will stop the spread of the virus so  what does the data tell us about the  scale of non-compliance  this chart shows the number of journeys  planned on apple maps in the last year  and you can see it plummeted during the  first lockdown  and then over here it fell again during  the second lockdown  it's now at its lowest point since april  which is roughly what you'd expect given  the strictness of the rules  a lot more things open now than they  were then  of course this data doesn't tell us  where people were traveling to  whether going to illegal raves or  dropping their kids off at nursery  for that we can use survey data and  fortunately we have an amazing resource  the covet 19 social study which  regularly asks more than 70  000 people about their experience of  lockdown  these are the headline results on  compliance  and this yellow line here represents  complete compliance  basically more than half of people say  they follow the rules  all the time this blue line up here is  majority compliance  it's really high 96 percent because  almost  everyone says they follow the rules  almost  all the time the gap between the two is  where people  bend the rules and add their own tweaks  and modifications  and yeah there's definitely quite a bit  of that but stepping back  this is a picture of really good  compliance and in fact  due to recent rises compliance is now at  its  highest level since april  now not everyone complies in the same  way  and i just want to highlight one fact  for you there's so much interesting  detail here for example  men comply much less than women but this  is really important  key workers say they comply 49 of the  time that is  10 percentage points less than non-key  workers  now that is something the government  really might look at especially now the  definition of key worker  is so much bigger thanks to this amazing  study we also have data on which  rules people break the yellow bit here  is when people say they never follow a  particular rule  and you can see the rules that people  are most likely to break  are meeting up outdoors and also  meeting up indoors which is let's know  much more dangerous but again  the important thing here isn't the small  amount of yellow  it's this very large amount of green  the data suggests that most people are  following the rules  it does make you wonder how much effect  will cracking down the small minority  have  on the spread of the virus compared to  just changing the rules  for everyone
 

